course: dictionary
lesson: 15598
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words look the same but mean different things, so the clues tell you which meaning is right. «Tai chi helps Grandma keep her balance in the park.» Here tai is part of a named exercise, so it means that slow, calm kind of exercise.
explain: A capital letter is a clue too. «Tai is my friend's first name.» With a capital T, Tai is the name of a person, not an English word. Names are proper nouns: they name one particular person, place or group.
explain: Words from other languages can look like English but mean something else. «Tài hǎo le!» is Mandarin, and tài means "very" or "too". Ask: what would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Tai is a proper noun: it has a capital letter and names something particular.
fact: Tai chi is a Chinese system of slow, flowing exercise movements, practised for health and calm.
fact: The Tai peoples and Tai languages include Thai and Lao, spoken in South-East Asia and southern China.
fact: In Mandarin, «tài» means "too" or "very", as in «tài lěng le» (too cold).
fact: Tai is not an English word on its own, so use a dictionary to check a name or a word from another language.
exercise: «Tai chi helps Grandma keep her balance in the park.» What is tai chi? | A kind of small boat | A type of cold food | A fast running race | *A calm, slow exercise
exercise: «Tai is my friend's first name.» What kind of word is Tai here? | A word for a colour | *A name for a person | A verb meaning to run | A word for the sea
exercise: In Mandarin, «tài» means what? | Hello and goodbye | Blue | *Too or very | Yes or no
test: Which languages are Tai languages? | Spanish and Italian | English and French | *Thai and Lao
test: Why does Tai with a capital letter mean a name? | It is the Mandarin word for hello | Capital letters only mark the first word of a sentence | *Capital letters often mark names, which are proper nouns | It is a verb that means to jump
exam: In «tài lěng le» (too cold), what does tài tell you about cold? | It is a name for the cold | It means the opposite of cold, meaning warm | It turns cold into a colour | *It makes cold stronger, meaning very cold
